<!-- MATH 14 Skeletal Notes                                                -->
<!-- Section: Green's Theorem in the Plane                                 -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Skeletal (fill-in) version of the section of the same name in the     -->
<!-- completed MATH 14 notes.  The definitions of divergence and curl, the -->
<!-- two statements of Green's theorem, and the component test with the   -->
<!-- curl determinant are kept; the flux and circulation computations     -->
<!-- around the small rectangle, the proof of the tangential form, and the -->
<!-- four worked examples are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each      -->
<!-- preceded by a one-line instruction.  File name and xml:ids are        -->
<!-- unchanged, so the assignments and the later sections still cite       -->
<!-- eq-flux-density, eq-div-def, eq-curl-def, eq-green-normal,           -->
<!-- eq-green-tangential and eq-component-test-3d.                        -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Figures: all kept (the two small rectangles, the four field plots,   -->
<!-- the proof region, the unit circle, the tilted square and every        -->
<!-- video); each is the check on the box above it.  Captions that stated  -->
<!-- a value or a substitution have been trimmed.                          -->
<section xml:id="section-greens-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Green's Theorem in the Plane</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Before we learn about Green's theorem, we will need to learn about two
operations on vector fields: <term>curl</term> and
<term>divergence</term>. We begin by studying divergence.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-divergence">
<title>Divergence</title>

<p>
Consider a vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> to be
the velocity field of a fluid. How can we find the net flow rate of the
gas through the boundaries of a rectangle shown in
<xref ref="fig-div-flux"/>?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-flux">
<caption>
The flux of the field <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>
through the four sides of a small rectangle with corner <m>(x,y)</m>,
width <m>\Delta x</m>, and height <m>\Delta y</m>. Each side's outward
component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is drawn in its own color, and the legend
gives the dot product it represents.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<shortdescription>
A rectangle in a vector field with a differently colored outward arrow through each of its four sides, and a legend naming the gray field arrows and pairing each color with its dot product.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-flux">
<diagram dimensions="(580,300)" margins="10">
<definition>field(x,y) = (0.4*x + 0.25, 0.32*y + 0.18)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.6,-0.6,8.7,4.6)">
<vector-field at="df-field" function="field"
              spacings="((1.8,1.8,5.5),(0.15,1.3,4.1))" scale="0.36"
              stroke="#bbbbbb"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="df-rect" points="((1.2,1.0),(4.8,1.0),(4.8,3.2),(1.2,3.2))"
         stroke="#2b9ad6" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="df-corner-bl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,1.0)" fill="#d02090" size="3"
       alignment="sw">
<m>(x,y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-br" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,1.0)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-tl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="nw">
<m>(x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-tr" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="ne">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<line at="df-top" p1="(3.0,3.2)" p2="(3.0,3.75)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-bottom" p1="(3.0,1.0)" p2="(3.0,0.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-right" p1="(4.8,2.1)" p2="(5.35,2.1)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-left" p1="(1.2,2.1)" p2="(0.65,2.1)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#7b3294" thickness="3"/>
<!-- Legend: first the field itself (the gray arrows), then one row   -->
<!-- per side, a swatch arrow in the side's color paired with the dot  -->
<!-- product it represents, in the order of the derivation (top,       -->
<!-- bottom, right, left) in the text below the figure.               -->
<line at="df-leg-field" p1="(-5.45,4.35)" p2="(-5.05,4.35)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#bbbbbb" thickness="2"/>
<label at="df-leg-field-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,4.35)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</label>
<line at="df-leg-top" p1="(-5.45,3.90)" p2="(-5.05,3.90)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="df-leg-top-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.90)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{j}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<line at="df-leg-bottom" p1="(-5.45,3.45)" p2="(-5.05,3.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="df-leg-bottom-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.45)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{j})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<line at="df-leg-right" p1="(-5.45,3.00)" p2="(-5.05,3.00)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<label at="df-leg-right-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.00)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{i}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<line at="df-leg-left" p1="(-5.45,2.55)" p2="(-5.05,2.55)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#7b3294" thickness="3"/>
<label at="df-leg-left-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,2.55)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{i})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="df-A" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,2.35)" alignment="c">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="df-dx" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,0.05)" alignment="s">
<m>\Delta x</m>
</label>
<label at="df-dy" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.95,2.1)" alignment="e">
<m>\Delta y</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue rectangle with corner x comma y, width delta x, and height delta y, sitting in a gray vector field that points up and to the right. An arrow at the middle of each side shows the outward component of the field in that side's own color, and a legend at the upper left names the gray arrows as the field F and pairs each color with its dot product: positive through the top and right sides, negative through the bottom and left sides.">
  <annotation ref="df-rect" text="The rectangle with area A equals delta x delta y through whose boundary we measure the net flux."/>
  <annotation ref="df-top" text="On the top side, in vermillion, the outward direction is j, and F dot j is positive: fluid leaves through the top."/>
  <annotation ref="df-right" text="On the right side, in green, the outward direction is i, and F dot i is positive: fluid leaves through the right."/>
  <annotation ref="df-bottom" text="On the bottom side, in orange, the outward direction is negative j, and F dot negative j is negative: fluid enters through the bottom."/>
  <annotation ref="df-left" text="On the left side, in purple, the outward direction is negative i, and F dot negative i is negative: fluid enters through the left."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Evaluate the flux through each side, assuming the field is nearly
constant along each side: on each side take the outward component of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> times the length of the side, with <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
evaluated at the side's corner shown in the figure. Then add the four
contributions, pair the parallel sides, and use the linear
approximations
<m>M(x+\Delta x, y) - M(x,y) \approx \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}\,\Delta x</m>
and <m>N(x, y+\Delta y) - N(x,y) \approx \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}\,\Delta y</m>
to write the net flow rate.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Top: \qquad Bottom: \qquad Right: \qquad Left:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {top $+$ bottom $\approx$ \qquad\qquad right $+$ left $\approx$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {net flow rate $\approx$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the flux through each of the four sides, for the sums over the two pairs of parallel sides, and for the net flow rate out of the rectangle.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Note that this is an approximation, since we are assuming the velocity
field has a constant value along each side of the rectangle. The
<term>flux density</term> can be calculated as follows:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-flux-density">
\text{Flux density}
= \frac{\text{net flux through the rectangle}}
{\text{area of the rectangle}}
= \frac{\left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial N}{\partial y} \right) \Delta x \Delta y}
{\Delta x \Delta y}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}.
</men>

<p>
Note that, in the limit that <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta y</m>
approach <m>0</m>, <xref ref="eq-flux-density"/> represents to what
extent a gas
is expanding or compressing at each point. This quantity is known as
the <term>divergence</term>, and it is denoted by
<m>\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F}</m>. Watch the construction in
<xref ref="fig-div-video"/>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-divergence">
<statement>
<p>
The divergence of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
defined as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-div-def">
\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y},
</men>

<p>
where <m>\nabla = \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}\,\mathbf{i}
+ \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}\,\mathbf{j}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-video">
<caption>
The net flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> out of a small rectangle, divided by
its area, converges to the divergence
<m>\partial M/\partial x + \partial N/\partial y</m> as the rectangle
shrinks.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-divergence"
       source="videos/divergence_flux_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-divergence-fields">
<title>Computing and Interpreting Divergence</title>
<statement>
<p>
The vector fields below represent the velocity field of a gas.
Calculate the divergence in each case and explain what it means
physically.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{-y}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{i}
+ \dfrac{x}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Apply <xref ref="eq-div-def"/> to each field, and say what the sign
of the result means for the gas at every point. The two fields are
drawn in <xref ref="fig-div-fields"/>; does the picture agree?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {A. $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F =$ \qquad meaning:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {B. $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F =$ \qquad meaning:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two labelled rows, one for the divergence of each field with its physical meaning.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-fields">
<caption>
Left: the field <m>2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>, which flows
away from the origin. Right: the field <m>\dfrac{-y\,\mathbf{i} +
x\,\mathbf{j}}{x^2+y^2}</m>, which circulates around the origin.
Compare with the divergences you computed.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<shortdescription>
A radial field with arrows pointing away from the origin.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-field-A">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<definition>fieldA(x,y) = (2*x, 2*y)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-3.4,3.4,3.4)">
<axes at="dfa-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector-field at="dfa-field" function="fieldA"
              spacings="((-3,1,3),(-3,1,3))" stroke="#2b6cb0"/>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The vector field 2 x i plus 2 y j. All arrows point radially away from the origin and grow longer farther from it. The divergence is 4 at every point: the gas expands.">
  <annotation ref="dfa-field" text="Arrows pointing radially outward from the origin, longer as the distance grows."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
A rotational field circulating counterclockwise around the origin.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-field-B">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<definition>fieldB(x,y) = (-y/(x^2 + y^2 + 0.2), x/(x^2 + y^2 + 0.2))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-3.4,3.4,3.4)">
<axes at="dfb-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector-field at="dfb-field" function="fieldB"
              spacings="((-3,1,3),(-3,1,3))" stroke="#2b6cb0"/>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The vector field negative y i plus x j, divided by x squared plus y squared. Arrows circulate counterclockwise around the origin, shorter as the distance grows. The divergence is 0 at every point.">
  <annotation ref="dfb-field" text="Arrows circulating counterclockwise around the origin, weaker farther away."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-curl">
<title>Curl</title>

<p>
Again, let us consider a vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> to be
the velocity field of a fluid. This time we are interested in answering
this question: how can we figure out the <term>circulation
density</term> of the fluid at any point on the <m>xy</m>-plane?
</p>

<p>
To answer this question, we compute the flow rates of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> <em>along</em> the sides of the rectangle
(counterclockwise), as in <xref ref="fig-curl-circulation"/>, and then
we take the limit as <m>\Delta x, \Delta y \to 0</m> to find the
circulation density at a point in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-circulation">
<caption>
The counterclockwise circulation of
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> around the rectangle:
along each side we take the component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> in the
direction of travel. Each side's component is drawn in its own color,
and the legend gives the dot product it represents.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<shortdescription>
A rectangle in a vector field with differently colored arrows tracing a counterclockwise loop along its sides, and a legend naming the gray field arrows and pairing each color with its dot product.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curl-circulation">
<diagram dimensions="(580,300)" margins="10">
<definition>field(x,y) = (0.4*x + 0.25, 0.32*y + 0.18)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.6,-0.6,8.7,4.6)">
<vector-field at="cf-field" function="field"
              spacings="((1.8,1.8,5.5),(0.15,1.3,4.1))" scale="0.36"
              stroke="#bbbbbb"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="cf-rect" points="((1.2,1.0),(4.8,1.0),(4.8,3.2),(1.2,3.2))"
         stroke="#2b9ad6" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="cf-corner-bl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,1.0)" fill="#d02090" size="3"
       alignment="sw">
<m>(x,y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-br" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,1.0)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-tl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="nw">
<m>(x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-tr" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="ne">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<line at="cf-top" p1="(3.2,3.2)" p2="(2.65,3.2)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-bottom" p1="(2.8,1.0)" p2="(3.35,1.0)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-right" p1="(4.8,1.9)" p2="(4.8,2.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-left" p1="(1.2,2.3)" p2="(1.2,1.75)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#7b3294" thickness="3"/>
<!-- Legend: first the field itself (the gray arrows), then one row   -->
<!-- per side, a swatch arrow in the side's color paired with the dot  -->
<!-- product it represents, in the order of the derivation (top,       -->
<!-- bottom, right, left) in the text below the figure.               -->
<!-- The side colors match the flux figure earlier in this section.    -->
<line at="cf-leg-field" p1="(-5.45,4.35)" p2="(-5.05,4.35)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#bbbbbb" thickness="2"/>
<label at="cf-leg-field-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,4.35)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</label>
<line at="cf-leg-top" p1="(-5.45,3.90)" p2="(-5.05,3.90)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cf-leg-top-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.90)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{i})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<line at="cf-leg-bottom" p1="(-5.45,3.45)" p2="(-5.05,3.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cf-leg-bottom-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.45)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{i}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<line at="cf-leg-right" p1="(-5.45,3.00)" p2="(-5.05,3.00)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cf-leg-right-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,3.00)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{j}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<line at="cf-leg-left" p1="(-5.45,2.55)" p2="(-5.05,2.55)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#7b3294" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cf-leg-left-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(-4.95,2.55)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{j})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-A" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,2.35)" alignment="c">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-dx" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,0.05)" alignment="s">
<m>\Delta x</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-dy" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.95,2.1)" alignment="e">
<m>\Delta y</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same blue rectangle in the same gray vector field, but now the arrows point along the sides, tracing a counterclockwise loop: rightward along the bottom, up the right side, leftward along the top, and down the left side. Each side's arrow has its own color, the same color as in the flux figure, and a legend at the upper left names the gray arrows as the field F and pairs each color with its dot product.">
  <annotation ref="cf-rect" text="The rectangle around which we measure the counterclockwise circulation."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-bottom" text="Along the bottom, in orange, the direction of travel is i, and F dot i is positive: the field pushes with the loop."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-right" text="Up the right side, in green, the direction of travel is j, and F dot j is positive."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-top" text="Along the top, in vermillion, the direction of travel is negative i, and F dot negative i is negative: the field pushes against the loop."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-left" text="Down the left side, in purple, the direction of travel is negative j, and F dot negative j is negative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Compute the flow rate along each edge, taking the component of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> in the direction of travel (counterclockwise) times
the length of the edge, with <m>\mathbf{F}</m> evaluated at the corner
shown in the figure. Sum the flows along the two pairs of parallel
edges, using the same linear approximations as for the divergence, and
divide by the area of the rectangle to get the circulation density.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Top: \qquad Bottom: \qquad Right: \qquad Left:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {top $+$ bottom $\approx$ \qquad\qquad right $+$ left $\approx$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\dfrac{\text{circulation around rectangle}}{\text{rectangle area}} \approx$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the flow along each of the four edges, for the sums over the two pairs of parallel edges, and for the circulation density.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
This quantity is denoted by
<m>\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}</m> and is known as
the <term><m>k</m>-component of curl</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-curl-k">
<statement>
<p>
The <m>k</m>-component of the curl of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-curl-def">
\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}
= \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
To find out the circulation density at a point in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, you can imagine a <term>paddle wheel</term> whose axis
goes through the point of interest. Then the <m>k</m>-component of curl
is positive if the fluid flow causes the paddle wheel to rotate
counterclockwise, and it is negative if it turns clockwise. The
animation in <xref ref="fig-curl-video"/> shows both behaviors.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-video">
<caption>
The counterclockwise circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around a small
rectangle, divided by its area, converges to the <m>k</m>-component of
curl. A paddle wheel placed in the fluid spins counterclockwise where
the curl is positive.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-curl"
       source="videos/curl_circulation_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-curl-fields">
<title>Computing and Interpreting Curl</title>
<statement>
<p>
The vector fields below represent the velocity field of a fluid.
Calculate the curl in each case and explain what it means physically.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = -2y\,\mathbf{i} + 2x\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Identify <m>M</m> and <m>N</m>, apply <xref ref="eq-curl-def"/>, and
say what a paddle wheel placed in the fluid would do. The two fields
are drawn in <xref ref="fig-curl-fields"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {A. $\operatorname{curl}\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf k =$ \qquad the paddle wheel:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {B. $\operatorname{curl}\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf k =$ \qquad the paddle wheel:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two labelled rows, one for the k component of the curl of each field with its physical meaning.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-fields">
<caption>
Left: the radial field <m>2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>. Right:
the rotational field <m>-2y\,\mathbf{i} + 2x\,\mathbf{j}</m>. Compare
with the curls you computed: would a paddle wheel spin in each?
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
