</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-greens-theorem-forms">
<title>The Two Forms of Green's Theorem</title>

<p>
Next we state Green's theorem in 2D<fn>Note that we only cover Green's
theorem in 2D; however, you should know that this theorem can be
generalized to 3D, and in that case it is known as Stokes' theorem,
which is <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem"/>.</fn>, in two forms. The first form relates the outward flux
across a simple<fn>A curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane is
<term>simple</term> if it does not cross itself.</fn> closed curve
<m>C</m> and the double integral of the divergence over the region
<m>R</m> bounded by the curve <m>C</m>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-green-normal">
<title>Green's Theorem (Flux-Divergence or Normal Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a piecewise smooth, simple closed curve enclosing a
region <m>R</m> in the plane. Let
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> be a vector field
with <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> having continuous first partial derivatives
in an open region containing <m>R</m>. Then the outward flux of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>C</m> equals the double integral of
<m>\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F}</m> over the region <m>R</m> enclosed
by <m>C</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-green-normal">
\underbrace{\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n}\, ds
= \oint_C M\, dy - N\, dx}_{\text{outward flux through the boundary}}
= \underbrace{\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial N}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy}_{\text{double integral
of divergence over } R}.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The second form of Green's theorem connects the counterclockwise
circulation of a vector field around a simple closed curve <m>C</m>
and the double integral of the <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl
of the field over the region <m>R</m> enclosed by the curve.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-green-tangential">
<title>Green's Theorem (Circulation-Curl or Tangential Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a piecewise smooth, simple closed curve enclosing a
region <m>R</m> in the plane. Let
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> be a vector field
with <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> having continuous first partial derivatives
in an open region containing <m>R</m>. Then the counterclockwise
circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around <m>C</m> equals the double
integral of <m>(\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F}) \cdot \mathbf{k}</m>
over <m>R</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-green-tangential">
\underbrace{\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{T}\, ds
= \oint_C M\, dx + N\, dy}_{\text{counterclockwise circulation}}
= \underbrace{\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy}_{\text{double integral
of curl} \,\cdot\, \mathbf{k} \text{ over } R}.
</men>
</statement>

<proof xml:id="proof-green-tangential">
<p>
(Both sides of <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/> split into a piece
carrying <m>M</m> and a piece carrying <m>N</m>, so it is enough to
prove the two statements
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-green-two" number="yes">
\oint_C M\,dx \amp = -\iint_R \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}\,dA ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\oint_C N\,dy \amp = \iint_R \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}\,dA ,
</mrow>
</md>
and add them. <em>Step 1</em>: for a region of the kind in
<xref ref="sec-general-regions-case1"/>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-green-proof-prefigure"/>, say why the vertical sides
contribute nothing to <m>\oint_C M\,dx</m>, write the bottom and top
contributions as integrals in <m>x</m> (mind the direction of travel
along the top), and compare with the double integral done in the
<m>y</m>-direction first, where the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus
evaluates the inner integral. <em>Step 2</em>: read <m>R</m> the other
way, as in <xref ref="sec-general-regions-case2"/>, and repeat for
<m>N</m>; say why no minus sign appears this time. <em>Step 3</em>:
say what to do with a region that cannot be described both ways, and
why the cuts cancel; <xref ref="fig-green-proof-video"/> shows the
idea. Finally, explain how <xref ref="thm-green-normal"/> follows by
applying the tangential form to the field
<m>\langle -N, M\rangle</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,14) -- (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {Step 1: $\displaystyle\oint_C M\,dx =$ \qquad and \qquad $\displaystyle\iint_R \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}\,dA =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {Step 2: $\displaystyle\oint_C N\,dy =$ \qquad and \qquad $\displaystyle\iint_R \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}\,dA =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {Step 3: general regions};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {the normal form from the tangential form, applied to $\langle -N, M\rangle$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the M statement on a region cut by vertical lines, for the N statement, for the extension to general regions, and for deriving the normal form from the tangential form.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-proof-prefigure">
<caption>
Step 1 of the proof of <xref ref="thm-green-tangential"/>: a region
<m>R</m> cut by vertical lines, with <m>f_2(x) \le y \le f_1(x)</m> for
<m>a \le x \le b</m>. Counterclockwise, the bottom curve is travelled left
to right and the top curve right to left; on the two vertical sides
<m>dx = 0</m>, so they contribute nothing to
<m>\oint_C M\,dx</m>.
</caption>
