</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-proof-video">
<caption>
The idea of the proof, without the algebra: the <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component
of the curl is circulation per unit area, so cover <m>R</m> with tiny
rectangles and add the circulation around each one. Every interior edge is
travelled twice, once in each direction, so the inside cancels in pairs and
only a staircase hugging <m>C</m> is left. As the number of rectangles
increases, less of their area sticks out of <m>R</m> and the
approximation improves; in the limit the staircase becomes <m>C</m> and
the sum becomes the double integral.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-green-proof-idea"
       source="videos/green_circulation_proof_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-green-circle">
<title>Verifying Both Forms on the Unit Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (x - y)\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> and the
closed curve <m>C</m> is the unit circle centered at the origin.
Convince yourself of the two forms of Green's theorem by applying them
to this example.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Parametrize the circle, write <m>M</m>, <m>N</m>, <m>dx</m> and
<m>dy</m> in terms of <m>t</m>, and compute the four partial
derivatives. For the normal form <xref ref="eq-green-normal"/>,
evaluate the line integral <m>\oint_C M\,dy - N\,dx</m> and the double
integral of the divergence separately and compare; for the tangential
form <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>, do the same with
<m>\oint_C M\,dx + N\,dy</m> and the curl. Both double integrals reduce
to the area of the disk. The field is shown in
<xref ref="fig-green-circle"/>; compare with
<xref ref="fig-green-circle-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,18);
\draw[gray!35] (0,14.5) -- (15,14.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12) -- (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,17.7) {$\mathbf r(t) =$ \qquad $M =$ \quad $N =$ \quad $dx =$ \quad $dy =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.2) {$\dfrac{\partial M}{\partial x} =$ \quad $\dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =$ \quad $\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x} =$ \quad $\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial y} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {normal form: $\displaystyle\oint_C M\,dy - N\,dx =$ \qquad $\displaystyle\iint_R \Big(\frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}\Big)dA =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {tangential form: $\displaystyle\oint_C M\,dx + N\,dy =$ \qquad $\displaystyle\iint_R \Big(\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}\Big)dA =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the parametrization with M, N, dx and dy, for the four partial derivatives, for the two sides of the normal form, and for the two sides of the tangential form.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-circle-video">
