<example xml:id="example-green-diamond">
<title>A Line Integral via Green's Theorem and Substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the line integral
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \frac{dx}{x+y} - \frac{dy}{x+y},
</me>

<p>
where <m>C</m> is the boundary of the square with vertices
<m>(1,0)</m>, <m>(2,1)</m>, <m>(1,2)</m>, and <m>(0,1)</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-green-diamond"/>. Make suitable substitutions in your
integral to make the limits of integration numbers as opposed to
functions.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Identify <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> and compute
<m>\partial N/\partial x - \partial M/\partial y</m>. Use the
tangential form <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/> to turn the line
integral into a double integral over the square <m>R</m>. Write the
equations of the four sides of the square; they suggest the
substitution <m>u</m>, <m>v</m> that turns <m>R</m> into a rectangle
with constant limits, as in <xref ref="fig-green-diamond"/>. Solve for
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, compute the Jacobian, and evaluate. Compare with
<xref ref="fig-green-diamond-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13) -- (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,15.7) {$M =$ \quad $N =$ \qquad $\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$\displaystyle\oint_C M\,dx + N\,dy = \iint_R$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {the four sides: \qquad so $u =$ \quad $v =$ \qquad and $R$ becomes};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {$x =$ \quad $y =$ \qquad $\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {evaluate:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for M, N and the curl, for the double integral given by Green's theorem, for the substitution suggested by the sides, for the inverse and the Jacobian, and for the evaluation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

